        "summary": "Tulsa Studies in Women’s Literature prohibits submissions produced through generative AI, including AI used to write text, survey criticism, or generate ideas. The journal allows AI-generated images or translated passages only with clear attribution when appropriate, and grammar or spelling tools are acceptable only if they do not substantially rewrite the author's prose; any AI-made wording changes must be clearly cited.",
        "evidence": "As a journal of literary criticism dedicated to the analysis of the written word, Tulsa Studies in Women’s Literature will not consider submissions produced through generative artificial intelligence. This prohibition includes but is not limited to the use of artificial intelligence to write text, survey preexisting criticism, or generate ideas.\n\nAI generated images or translated passages may be included with clear attribution when appropriate, and the author assumes all responsibility for the accuracy of these inclusions. Online tools that correct grammar or spelling are also acceptable, provided that the tool does not substantially rewrite an author’s prose; any changes made to wording by an AI tool must be clearly cited in the article.",
