        "domain": "Teaching & Learning",
        "summary": "AI use for learning support is addressed at the course level rather than by a blanket student rule. Faculty are encouraged to specify whether AI is allowed, discouraged, or expected for learning, and one sample policy explicitly says AI tools should be used critically to deepen subject matter understanding.",
        "evidence": "The course syllabus should lay out an overall AI-use policy that applies to your course. Just as you may allow or disallow the use of devices in class, appraise your students of AU’s academic integrity code, or share your teaching philosophy and expectations, draft a statement that informs your students about allowable, encouraged, or expected AI-use.\nAI is conditionally allowed: “In this course discourages but allows the use of AI. When using AI tools on assignments, add an appendix documenting (a) key passage of your AI exchange, (b) which AI tools were used, and (c) how AI tools were used (e.g. to generate ideas, turns of phrase, elements of text, etc.). AI tools should be used critically with an aim to deepen subject matter understanding. Use the ‘study mode’ setting whenever possible.”\nAI is encouraged for learning: “In this course, you are expected to use AI (e.g., ChatGPT and image generation tools), and some assignments will require you to use AI tools. Learning to use AI is an emerging skill, the acquisition of which this course supports through examples and tutorials. Use the ‘study mode’ setting when relevant.”",
