        "summary": "Seattle University treats research integrity issues under its research misconduct framework, which covers plagiarism, fabrication, and falsification in proposing, performing, reviewing, or reporting research. Its AI research guidance also says human-subjects ethics review should address AI-related data privacy and protection. The provided sources do not contain a specific rule about AI use in grant proposals or IRB applications beyond those general research integrity and privacy requirements.",
        "evidence": "Research misconduct. Research misconduct means fabrication, falsification, or plagiarism in proposing, performing, or reviewing research, or in reporting research results. Research misconduct does not include honest error or differences of opinion.\n\nPlagiarism. Plagiarism means the appropriation of another person’s ideas, processes, results, or words, without giving appropriate credit.\n\nResearchers should avoid sharing sensitive personal data with AI tools, as those tools may leak the data collected for training or context-building purposes. Thus, human subjects ethics review should incorporate a component of data privacy and protection vis-à-vis AI tools.",
