        "domain": "Research",
        "summary": "Adelphi’s research guidance requires disclosure and prior review when AI is used in human-subjects research activities. The IRB manual says researchers must describe proposed AI use in IRB submissions, and IRB approval is required before AI tools are used to collect, analyze, or generate data involving human participants. It also bars uploading identifiable or confidential research information into public AI tools without IRB approval and safeguards.",
        "evidence": "Researchers proposing to use artificial intelligence (AI) tools in the design, recruitment, data collection, data analysis, or dissemination phases of human subjects research must describe such use in their IRB application.\n\nThe IRB must review and approve any use of AI that may affect the rights, welfare, privacy, or consent capacity of human participants.\n\nResearchers must not upload identifiable private information, protected health information, confidential data, or unpublished research data into publicly available AI tools unless such use is explicitly approved by the IRB and appropriate safeguards are in place.\n\nUse of AI tools in human subjects research without prior IRB review, when required, may constitute noncompliance with university policy and federal regulations.",
