      "U10": {
        "classification_name": "Faculty & Staff Use",
        "domain": "Institutional & Administrative",
        "summary": "Faculty and staff are permitted to use AI for teaching preparation, student support, communications, and administrative efficiency. Faculty examples include creating tailored feedback, brainstorming quizzes and assignments, and drafting communications; staff examples include drafting and summarizing emails, transcribing meeting notes, automating data entry, drafting policy documents, and summarizing reports. Faculty are also advised to disclose any intended AI use in assessing student work.",
        "evidence": "Teaching and Learning\n* Personalized learning: Create tailored content, feedback and guidance for students based on their individual needs and learning styles.\n* Content creation: Brainstorm materials such as quizzes, rubrics and assignments.\n* Communication: Draft emails, announcements, event promotion and other communications.\n● Guideline 3: Disclose any intended use of AI in the assessment of student work and ask students to opt in or out to having their work added to a public database in this way.\nAI is transforming how we manage workflows, communicate and make decisions. Whether streamlining administrative processes, enhancing collaboration or supporting professional growth, AI can help staff work more efficiently while improving services and operations across the university.\nAI can be used to draft and summarize emails, transcribe meeting notes and automate data entry. Creating reports, drafting policy documents and organizing information more efficiently, allowing staff to focus on high-priority tasks.",
        "sourceUrls": [
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/use-cases/faculty",
          "https://academicaffairs.appstate.edu/sites/default/files/app_state_ai_syllabus_guidance.pdf",
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/use-cases/staff"
        ],
        "defined": true
      },
      "U11": {
        "classification_name": "Institutional Data Protection & Approved AI Platforms",
        "domain": "Institutional & Administrative",
        "summary": "The university requires use of reviewed and contracted AI tools for university information and maintains approved tool lists with categories such as university-provided, available upon request, and approved but not supported. Public or published university information may be used with AI tools, but sensitive or confidential information must not be entered into unapproved systems and, in some cases, must not be used with any AI tools. Free or non-App State-managed versions of tools such as ChatGPT are explicitly identified as unauthorized when they lack a university contract or data-sharing controls.",
        "evidence": "Faculty, students and staff may use university information with generative AI tools or services only when:\n* The AI tool or service being used has undergone appropriate internal reviews and contract terms are in place to protect data assets.\nITS strives to thoroughly vet and approve AI tools for university use based on data privacy and protection measures, including:\n* Secure handling and storage: When using AI tools, personal and institutional data should be securely managed and not shared with third parties.\n* Data retention: When prompts, responses or uploaded files are submitted to an AI tool, they may be copied or retained by the LLM provider. ITS encourages the use of tools that do not use university data to train public models.\nReminder: Even with these precautions, data privacy and security remain paramount. Do not enter sensitive or confidential information into any AI chat interface that has not been approved for use with sensitive or confidential data.\n* Unauthorized AI tools: Tools that lack a university contract, lack appropriate data-sharing controls. This includes free or non-App State-managed versions of AI tools like ChatGPT.\n* Sensitive information: Student records subject to FERPA, health information, proprietary information and any other data classified as confidential must not be used with any AI tools.\nThese lists include AI tools that have been reviewed and are currently available for use in one of the following ways:\n*  As a university-provided and supported tool\n* As a university-provided tool available upon request\n* Other university-approved, but not supported tools",
        "sourceUrls": [
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/guidance/policies",
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/guidance/data-security",
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/tools"
        ],
        "defined": true
      },
      "U12": {
        "classification_name": "University AI Governance & Strategy",
        "domain": "Institutional & Administrative",
        "summary": "Appalachian State has a formal AI governance structure centered on the Chancellor’s Artificial Intelligence Task Force and other AI advisory groups. The task force is cross-divisional and is charged with assessing AI’s role across teaching, research, service, and administrative operations, while developing strategic guidance and recommendations for responsible use and stronger safeguards for security and privacy. The AI Hub is also described as a living site that will continue to evolve as university work on AI progresses.",
        "evidence": "Welcome to the AI Hub for App State, a centralized location for AI tools, training and resources to support teaching and learning, research and administrative work for students, faculty and staff. This is a living site, which means we will be continuously updating and evolving to respond to user needs and changing information.\nThe Chancellor’s Artificial Intelligence Task Force (AITF) is a cross-divisional team committed to assessing the evolving role of AI in teaching, research, service and administrative operations at Appalachian State University.\nChaired by Chief Information Officer Keith Werner, the AITF will develop strategic guidance and recommendations for the responsible use of AI across campus while enhancing safeguards for institutional security and individual privacy.\nThe Chief Information Officer serves as the Chair of the AITF. Existing AI advisory groups, notably the Provost's AI Task Force, are actively engaged in investigating the responsible use of AI.  Academic Affairs also currently has AI advisory groups for respective divisions. These, and other advisory groups as they are developed, will inform the AITF.",
        "sourceUrls": [
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/",
          "https://ai.appstate.edu/guidance/aitf"
        ],
        "defined": true
      }
