        "domain": "Institutional & Administrative",
        "summary": "Arcadia provides guidance to faculty and staff rather than mandatory uniform operational rules. Faculty are recommended to decide AI use by course context and to communicate permitted and prohibited uses in syllabi; the library guide also points faculty to sample policies and further learning resources.",
        "evidence": "Because generative AI has a range of uses and limitations which vary across disciplines, classroom environments, learning opportunities, and tasks, we recommend each faculty member consider AI uses and limitations in the context of each course. You might use AI liberally in one course, selectively in another, or not at all.\nWe encourage faculty to include a statement in each course syllabus explaining what AI uses are and are not permitted, as this could be different for each class. This may even vary among assignments; the syllabus is a way to communicate that to students.\nTemple University and Colorado State University both have brief guides that present a variety of sample syllabus language that could be useful in various classroom contexts. Lance Eaton has compiled what appears to be the most comprehensive collection of sample policies and syllabus language to date.",
        "sourceUrls": [
          "https://www.arcadia.edu/academics/teaching-learning/guidance-for-generative-ai/",
          "https://www.arcadia.edu/academics/library/research-subject-guides/generative-ai-in-higher-education/"
        ],
        "defined": true
