        "domain": "Teaching & Learning",
        "summary": "The seminary states that assistive AI can be acceptable for limited help with learning-related tasks, such as finding Bible passages or researching background and meanings for sermon preparation. At the same time, it warns that reliance on AI and search tools may reduce memory and learning.",
        "evidence": "First, is “Assistive AI” that assists an individual. This seems to be acceptable.\nAI may also be acceptable in helping preachers find Bible passages.\nAlso, using AI to research the background of a biblical text or the meaning of biblical terms may be acceptable for assisting in sermon preparation.\nI mentioned earlier in this blog that using AI to help preachers find Bible passages, etc. is acceptable.\nHowever, according to the MIT report, “the reliance on search engines for information retrieval, known as the “Google Effect,” can shift cognitive efforts from information retention to more externalized memory processes . . .\nNamely, as users increasingly depend on search engines for fact-checking and accessing information, their ability to remember specific content may decline, although they retain a strong recall of how and where to find it.”",
