        "summary": "Bard does not provide a single university-wide permission rule for AI as a study aid; instead, use depends on the instructor’s course policy. The materials explicitly recognize that generative AI may be valuable in some educational contexts, and sample faculty statements show some instructors allowing AI as a tutor or for understanding complex topics if transparently cited.",
        "evidence": "We recognize that generative AI may serve valuable purposes in specific educational contexts. It is the responsibility of faculty to make clear if, when, and how GenAI will play a role in any particular course or assignment, and to set expectations, if applicable, for appropriate acknowledgement and/or citation of GenAI tools.\nThat said, there is doubtless a place for LLMs in this class—as a digital tutor, as a coach and question-asker, etc.\nI expect you to cite sources that you use, including tools that reword your writing like Grammarly, tutors who help you rework a homework or paper, or if you use chatGPT or similar tools to understand a complex topic.",
        "sourceUrls": ["https://www.bard.edu/wwwmedia/files/7137089/2/Bard%20College%20Policy%20on%20Academic%20Use%20of%20Generative%20Artificial%20Intelligence.pdf", "https://www.bard.edu/wwwmedia/files/7137089/5/Sample%20Statements%20on%20AI%20%28Fall%202025%29.pdf"],
