        "domain": "Academic Integrity",
        "summary": "Bard states that current AI-detection tools are not sufficiently accurate and warns about false positives, especially for non-native English speakers. For enforcement, AI misuse is handled under academic integrity processes, with instructor policies defining course expectations and sample statements showing penalties such as plagiarism consequences, reporting to the Academic Judiciary Board, no credit, course failure, or failure of a program.",
        "evidence": "There are currently no AI-detection tools that are sufficiently accurate; they all suffer from too many false positives and false negatives. Further, AI-detection tools more frequently produce false positives for non-native English speakers.\nWhen it comes to matters of academic integrity, the instructor’s GenAI policy defines the expectations for that course.\nAs a result, the biology faculty will consider the use of ChatGPT and similar AI programs to generate text – with attribution or not – to be a violation of academic integrity, with consequences for plagiarism and cheating.\nStudents caught plagiarizing will be reported to the Academic Judiciary Board, will get no credit for the assignment, and may fail the course.\nAny student who uses AI during L&T to generate writing—including freewrites or the final essay—will fail the program.",
        "sourceUrls": ["https://www.bard.edu/wwwmedia/files/7137089/3/Privacy%20considerations%20for%20Generative%20AI%20on%20Campus.pdf", "https://www.bard.edu/wwwmedia/files/7137089/2/Bard%20College%20Policy%20on%20Academic%20Use%20of%20Generative%20Artificial%20Intelligence.pdf", "https://www.bard.edu/wwwmedia/files/7137089/5/Sample%20Statements%20on%20AI%20%28Fall%202025%29.pdf"],
